  "question": {
    "text": "A bead of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;can slide without friction along a vertical ring of radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>. One end of a spring of force constant&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></math>&nbsp;is connected to the bead and the other&nbsp;end is fixed at the centre of the ring. Initially, the bead is at the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&nbsp;and due to a small push it starts sliding down the ring. If the bead momentarily loses contact with the ring at the instant when the spring makes an angle of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math>&nbsp;with the vertical, then the natural length of the spring is<br />\r<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0090_04b6cf9dcde1.jpg\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 115px;\" />"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>R</mi></mrow><mn>9</mn></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
